{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"wolflike-chevroned-haddock","creation_channel":"cli","description":"## Problem\n\nA provider 400 meaning \"your input is too big\" surfaces to the caller as\n`LLMUnavailableError: the LLM provider call failed` — provider-outage semantics for a\ndeterministic, caller-fixable input problem. The caller cannot tell \"retry later, the provider\nis down\" from \"shrink your input, this will never succeed\".\n\nObserved (scripted `ModelHTTPError`, offline):\n\n```\nModelHTTPError(400, \"prompt is too long: 206826 tokens > 200000 maximum\")\n  -> LLMUnavailableError: the LLM provider call failed\n```\n\n## Why this is a defect, not a design gap\n\nrebar **already computes the correct classification**. `run_failure.py:152` holds\n`CHANGE_INPUT` with `retryable=False` at the moment it wraps the error — and then wraps it in a\ntype whose name and message assert the opposite. Only the *type* is wrong; no new measurement,\nestimator, calibration, or classification work is required.\n\nConfirmed adjacent behaviour, so the blast radius is understood: there is **no** transport retry\n(400 is absent from `_RETRY_STATUSES`) and **no** fallback (`CHANGE_INPUT` is excluded), so\nnothing today depends on this presenting as an outage. But plan-review's size ladder burns a\nfull failed round-trip per rung against an error it cannot recognise as terminal-for-this-input.\n\n## Scope — deliberately narrow\n\nThis is a **type/message correction at an existing classification site**, not a new guard.\n\nExplicitly OUT of scope: any pre-flight measurement of the outgoing request. That is the\nshared-seam design change tracked by `undamaged-adolescent-sable` (`f79d-e364-5fdb-417d`), which\nneeds a token estimator and a calibration decision this ticket does not touch. This fix acts\nonly AFTER the provider has answered, so it is a **complement, not a substitute** — it cannot\nsatisfy that ticket's \"before the provider call\" requirement and does not claim to.\n\nAlso out of scope: widening which HTTP statuses are classified `CHANGE_INPUT`. Use the\nclassification already computed at the wrap site; do not add new ones.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] A provider 400 already classified `CHANGE_INPUT` at the wrap site raises an error whose\n      type and message identify it as a caller-fixable input problem, not a provider outage —\n      verified by a test scripting such an error offline and asserting on the raised type\n- [ ] The raised type remains a subclass of `LLMError`, so the 13 existing broad handlers keep\n      catching it — verified by a test asserting the subclass relationship\n- [ ] A genuine provider outage (a 5xx, or a transport failure) still raises\n      `LLMUnavailableError` — verified by a test, so the change discriminates rather than\n      relabelling everything\n- [ ] No change to `_RETRY_STATUSES` or to fallback eligibility — verified by a test pinning\n      that a `CHANGE_INPUT` 400 is still neither retried nor failed over","id":"43d4-b8f5-4bb2-417b","parent_id":"","priority":2,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"bug","title":"A CHANGE_INPUT 400 is raised as LLMUnavailableError, so a caller-fixable input problem reads as a provider outage"},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1787311946418380001,"uuid":"7fab90a8-22a1-450f-be01-5ce9ee668976"}